<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kahnawake</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caughnawaga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kahnawake is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> community on the south shore of the St Lawrence opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. It grew from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> mission founded in 1667 that drew Catholic Mohawks and other Iroquois from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kateri-tekakwitha">Kateri Tekakwitha</a>. Its people became famous in the 20th century as ironworkers on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skyscraper">skyscrapers</a> and bridges of New York and other cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kahnawake.jpg" alt="Kahnawake" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MTLskyline · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kahnawake_Quebec_location_diagram.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kahnawake lies on the south-west shore of the Saint Lawrence River where it narrows, across from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> word kahnawà꞉ke, place of the rapids, after the people's former village of Caughnawaga near the rapids of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-river">Mohawk River</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>; the nearby Lachine Rapids also shaped the choice. Its people call themselves Kanien’kehá꞉ka, People of the Flint, and their nation was historically the most easterly of the Haudenosaunee, the Keepers of the Eastern Door.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first Haudenosaunee settlement near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> was founded on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> land near La Prairie by a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a>, joined over the next decade by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> refugees from Ossernenon and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a>, Wendat and Algonquians. The village moved towards the Lachine Rapids in 1675, again in 1690 and 1696, and in 1715 to its present site. In 1680 the French Crown granted the Jesuits the Seigneurie du Sault-Saint-Louis, which was meant to be closed to European settlement, but the Jesuits let French settlers take land there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> kept a garrison at the village to guard the south-west approach to Ville-Marie.</p><p>In 1907 thirty-three Kahnawake men died in the collapse of the Quebec Bridge. In the first half of the 20th century many ironworkers moved with their families to Brooklyn, where they called their neighbourhood Little Caughnawaga. In 1955 the government expropriated 1264 acres for the Saint Lawrence Seaway, cutting the community off from the river despite its appeals to the courts and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. During the Oka Crisis of 1990 people from Kahnawake blocked the Honoré Mercier Bridge in support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesatake">Kanesatake</a>, and on 28 August 1990 a crowd stoned a convoy of women, children and elders leaving the reserve.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Kahnawà꞉ke Mohawk Territory is a self-governing First Nations reserve of 48.05 square kilometres, with a resident population slightly above 8,000 and many members living elsewhere. Its people, once speakers of Kanien'kéha and French, now mostly speak English. It holds three National Historic Sites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>: Fort St-Louis, the Jesuit Mission of St-François-Xavier and the Caughnawaga Presbytery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kahnawake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many Catholic converts moved to the mission villages of <b>Kahnawake</b> and Kanesatake near Montreal.</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li><li><blockquote>Jesuit missions drew Catholic converts north, among them Kateri Tekakwitha, to <b>Kahnawake</b> near Montreal.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohawk-nation">Mohawk Nation</a></li></ul></section>
